  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nMoisture transport will increase today bringing soupy dewpoints in \nthe 70s into the central part of the state. Heat indices will be \ngreatest the further south you travel in the state, with 95+ degree \nheat indices south of I-72. However, there could be some limitations \nwith the how high the heat indices may reach with the cloud cover \nfrom convective debris across the state and how fast the cold front \ntoday may move through. Currently, the front is located across \ncentral Iowa, extending north to northern Ontario. \n\nThe cold front will clear out the sweltering mid to upper 90 \ntemperatures, leaving highs in the low to mid 80s before building \nback to upper 80s to low 90s this weekend into early next week. The \nLREF suggests that there is a 40-70% chance of high temperatures \ncooler than 80 degrees by next Tuesday north of I-72.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will move through today as the front \npushes south and eventually stalls out across the southern half of \nthe state overnight. At the moment, there is an area of convection \nmoving east across the northern half of the state, scraping our \nnorthern most counties early this morning. This initial area of \nconvection will begin to push south as morning progresses and widely \ndry out late this morning into the afternoon hours. Scattered \nactivity will remain during the afternoon as the front pushes \nsoutheast through the state. This storms could have strong to \ndamaging winds within the strongest storms/clusters. \n\nRedevelopment may begin later than previous forecasts, as the CAMs \nhave the area of new convection moving into the southeast around 06z \ntonight. This convection looks to train near and south of I-72 along \nthe stalled front as the LLJ increases. The heaviest rain may be \nalong and south of the I-70 corridor. Another scenario that could \noccur, is that this redevelopment doesn't happen or is not nearly as \nwidespread, as depicted by the RRFS 09.00z run. Say the more \nwidespread redevelopment with heavy rain scenario plays out, it \nwould continue into Thursday morning and potentially have a low risk \nfor flash flooding, rather than severe threat. However, this area \ncan likely take quite a bit of rain before flooding becomes a major \nconcern. The 6 hour FFG for the southeast is 3-5 inches. PWATs in \nthat area are forecast to be 2.0-2.5 inches so very heavy rain is \npossible. Localized swaths of 1 to 3 inches are possible \n(particularly along and south of I-70) as highlighted by the HREF \nLPMM.\n\nWPC has maintained a D2 marginal risk ERO along/south of I-72. SPC \nhas shrunk the D2 marginal risk for severe weather near/south of 1-\n72. Damaging winds are the most likely hazard with the strongest \nstorms. \n\nAdditional low-end chances exist for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the weekend. Due to low predictability and model spread, \nbroad-brushed 20-40% PoPs remain in place from Friday night through \nearly next week.",
